WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 5.3 · Internal Systems & Operations
Processes, SOPs and process ownership

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Write down key processes so more than one person can do them, including those who join later.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:28.504
A process is a sequence of steps that produces a result, such as registering a participant or paying a supplier. A standard operating procedure (SOP) writes it down so more than one person can do it well.

4
00:00:28.904 --> 00:00:29.504
The parts of a process map

5
00:00:29.504 --> 00:00:40.093
Trigger: What starts the process, such as a request, a date or an order. For example: The youth centre refers a young person for a traineeship place.

6
00:00:40.093 --> 00:00:50.681
Steps and roles: What happens, in order, and who does each step. For example: One founder checks the age, explains the traineeship, collects consent and confirms a start date.

7
00:00:50.681 --> 00:01:00.900
Records: What is written down, and where it is kept. For example: A line in the trainee list and a signed consent form in the folder.

8
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:12.319
Exceptions: What happens when something goes wrong or does not fit. For example: No consent yet: the trainee waits. Group full: offered the next six-month intake.

9
00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:13.319
Why write it down

10
00:01:13.319 --> 00:01:18.622
1. Work gets done the same way, safely, whoever does it.

11
00:01:18.622 --> 00:01:22.815
2. Knowledge stops living in one founder's head.

12
00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:29.859
3. You cut steps that add nothing and add checks where mistakes cost.

13
00:01:30.259 --> 00:01:30.859
What it guides

14
00:01:30.859 --> 00:01:36.163
1. Your process register: each process, its owner and review date.

15
00:01:36.163 --> 00:01:41.096
2. Your RACI from Module 3: one owner per process.

16
00:01:41.096 --> 00:01:46.659
3. Your onboarding: newcomers learn from SOPs, not guesswork.

17
00:01:47.059 --> 00:01:47.659
What to avoid

18
00:01:47.659 --> 00:01:53.333
An SOP for everything, instead of what is frequent or risky.

19
00:01:53.333 --> 00:01:58.267
Describing the ideal process rather than what really happens.

20
00:01:58.267 --> 00:02:04.570
No owner, so the SOP quietly goes out of date.

21
00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:06.970
You start a process register and map one launch process.

22
00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:10.970
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:15.570
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
